   {
    "n": "07",
    "title": "Speech seasoned with salt",
    "blocks": [
     {
      "k": "p",
      "runs": [
       "Right after asking for prayer for his own open door, Paul turns to the Colossians’ everyday dealings with people who do not believe:"
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "quote",
      "r": "COL.4.5",
      "label": "Colossians 4:5",
      "t": "Act wisely toward outsiders, redeeming the time."
     },
     {
      "k": "quote",
      "r": "COL.4.6",
      "label": "Colossians 4:6",
      "t": "Let your speech always be gracious, seasoned with salt, so that you may know how to answer everyone."
     },
     {
      "k": "p",
      "runs": [
       {
        "b": "Grace"
       },
       " is God’s kindness given to people who have not earned it. Gracious speech carries some of that kindness. Salt gives flavour and keeps food from spoiling, so salty speech is neither dull nor rotten. To redeem the time is to buy up the moment and not waste it. That does not mean forcing every meeting into a sermon. It does mean not letting every meeting slip by."
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "p",
      "runs": [
       "“How to answer everyone” assumes that people differ. An answer is given to someone. It cannot be recited at them. One person wants to understand what you believe. Another is testing a claim. Another is telling you why trust has been hard for them. Those are this site’s examples; the verse does not sort people into kinds. But you cannot answer well without listening first."
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "p",
      "runs": [
       "Peter gives the same counsel to Christians under pressure:"
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "quote",
      "r": "1PE.3.15",
      "label": "1 Peter 3:15",
      "t": "But in your hearts sanctify Christ as Lord. Always be prepared to give a defense to everyone who asks you the reason for the hope that is in you. But respond with gentleness and respect,"
     },
     {
      "k": "p",
      "runs": [
       "To ",
       {
        "b": "sanctify"
       },
       " Christ here is to honour Him as holy, above every fear. Read more in ",
       {
        "s": "../faith-under-pressure-directions/",
        "t": "Faith under pressure"
       },
       ". Peter also says that a life lived honourably speaks: “Conduct yourselves with such honor among the Gentiles that, though they slander you as evildoers, they may see your good deeds and glorify God on the day He visits us” (",
       {
        "r": "1PE.2.12",
        "t": "1 Peter 2:12"
       },
       "). Read more in ",
       {
        "s": "../faith-in-daily-life-directions/",
        "t": "Faith in daily life"
       },
       ". The Baptist Faith and Message keeps both, in this order: “verbal witness undergirded by a Christian lifestyle” (article XI)."
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "p",
      "runs": [
       "Putting these passages together, here is this site’s practical counsel for telling the gospel. It is application, not command:"
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "list",
      "items": [
       {
        "label": "Listen first.",
        "runs": [
         "Learn what the person is actually asking."
        ]
       },
       {
        "label": "Use plain words.",
        "runs": [
         "Say “made right with God” before you say “justified”, or explain the word when you use it."
        ]
       },
       {
        "label": "Tell about Jesus, not only about yourself.",
        "runs": [
         "Say who He is, that He died for sins and rose, and that He is Lord (",
         {
          "r": "ROM.10.9",
          "t": "Romans 10:9"
         },
         "). Your story can show why it matters to you."
        ]
       },
       {
        "label": "Say what you do not know.",
        "runs": [
         "“I need to go and read about that” is more honest than bluffing. Peter asks for “the reason for the hope that is in you”, not an answer to every objection."
        ]
       },
       {
        "label": "Invite a response.",
        "runs": [
         "Ask what they think, and whether they would like to read a Gospel with you."
        ]
       },
       {
        "label": "Leave them free.",
        "runs": [
         "A person may say they have heard enough, and that must be honoured."
        ]
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "k": "limits",
      "runs": [
       "Gracious does not mean vague. The paragraph has just asked for the message to be declared “clearly” (",
       {
        "r": "COL.4.4",
        "t": "Colossians 4:4"
       },
       "). Salt is no licence for cutting remarks, and grace comes first in the verse."
      ]
     }
    ],
    "readings": [
     "COL.4.5-6"
    ],
    "also": [
     "1PE.3.15",
     "1PE.2.12"
    ],
    "consider": [
     "What are the three things ",
     {
      "r": "COL.4.5-6",
      "t": "Colossians 4:5–6"
     },
     " asks of believers toward outsiders? What does Peter say the answer is about?"
    ],
    "reflect": [
     "Write down, in five plain sentences, the reason for the hope you have in Christ. Mark one point you could explain well, and one question to study before you try."
    ]
   }
